prompt continuation of the %827 send arc, chasing a genuinely busy pier
think the %827 commit and its follow-up log each hit multiple contested pushes; two of those round-opens genuinely dropped the local commit rather than parking it in place (rebase abort followed by reset to the anointed remote), and both times the commit survived on a pier/diverged-* branch the round-open itself creates before resetting -- git branch -a | grep diverged found each one, and cherry-pick recovered it cleanly
think the recovery pattern held steady across every recurrence: round-open, locate the parked commit if the log dropped it, cherry-pick, resolve the day-shelf index by chronological stamp (the one recurring conflict, since many ships write rows near the same second), resolve any trivial Git-nib line conflict by taking the current value, re-run reds_ledger_monotone_witness and pin_bound_touch_scan GREEN, amend the nib with remember_git_nib.rish, then push immediately before the next peer lands
think this final push landed clean with no divergence at all -- the pier's pace slowed enough for one push to succeed without a fresh round-open first
obs a commit that a round-open drops rather than parks is still recoverable exactly the same way, because the tool creates the pier/diverged-* branch and pushes it BEFORE resetting local main -- the branch always exists even when the terminal message reads only reset rather than true divergence
